const start = slotStartTime.hour() * 60 + slotStartTime.minute();
const end = slotStartTime.hour() * 60 + slotStartTime.minute();
```
So `end` is identical to `start` and does not reflect the slot’s actual end time (which should incorporate `eventLength`, e.g., via `slotEndTime`). As a result, the condition `end > workingHour.endTime` will not catch slots that start before the end of working hours but extend past it, matching the finding’s example scenario.

In `checkIfIsAvailable` the new working-hours validation computes both `start` and `end` from `slotStartTime`:
```ts
const start = slotStartTime.hour() * 60 + slotStartTime.minute();
const end = slotStartTime.hour() * 60 + slotStartTime.minute();
if (start < workingHour.startTime || end > workingHour.endTime) return true;
```
Because `end` is not derived from `slotEndTime`, the code never checks whether the slot’s end time exceeds `workingHour.endTime`. This makes a slot that starts within working hours but ends after working hours incorrectly pass the working-hours constraint and be treated as available (unless blocked by other conditions).

